<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  Private interface between the LibreSCRS agent (D-Bus client) and a prompter
  binary (e.g. librescrs-pinentry-kde). The prompter claims the well-known
  name on the session bus; the agent's PrompterClient calls RequestSecret.
  The collected secret is returned as a sealed memfd (passed via SCM_RIGHTS,
  bypassing the dbus-daemon's message buffer for the secret bytes).
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.librescrs.Prompter1">
    <!--
      RequestSecret: ask the user for a single secret value.

      Inputs:
        kind     "pin" | "can" | "mrz" — selects widget and validation.
        options  optional metadata, recognised keys:
                   "title"       (s) prompt heading
                   "description" (s) explanatory text
                   "requester"   (s) human-readable client identity
                   "artifact"    (s) what the operation acts on (filename / hash)
                   "prompt_id"   (s) the identity this prompt answers to for
                                 the lifetime of the call — the value Cancel
                                 names to dismiss it. Opaque: the prompter
                                 stores it and never parses it. Absent only from
                                 a caller that cannot address its prompts, whose
                                 windows then cannot be dismissed by name.
                   "deadline_ms" (u) how long the holder gets, measured from
                                 the moment the window is SHOWN. A DURATION,
                                 not an absolute time, so no shared clock is
                                 needed and transport latency is not charged to
                                 the human. Absent or 0 means no deadline; a
                                 prompter must never read 0 as instant expiry.
                   "reader_model"     (s) the reader's shortened model string,
                                 e.g. "OMNIKEY 5422". TRUSTED, agent-owned.
                                 No interface wording is composed into it.
                   "reader_interface" (s) CLOSED vocabulary: "contact" |
                                 "contactless" | "unknown". The prompter says it
                                 in the holder's language; an unrecognised value
                                 is treated as "unknown". This is what separates
                                 the two slots of a dual-interface reader whose
                                 PC/SC names share a serial.
                   "reader_full"      (s) the literal PC/SC reader name, for a
                                 details affordance rather than the chrome.
                   "artifacts"   (as) UNTRUSTED per-document display names of a
                                 batch signing request; rendered client-supplied,
                                 in the description zone's plain-text treatment —
                                 never inside the trusted "artifact" framing.
                                 Absent for every prompt that is not a batch sign.
                   "min_length"  (u) numeric kinds
                   "max_length"  (u) numeric kinds
                   "attempt"     (u) retry context: which attempt this
                                 prompt is (2 = second attempt, ...); absent
                                 on the first-ever prompt for a card
                   "last_error"  (s) retry context: msgKey of the failure
                                 that triggered the retry; absent alongside
                                 "attempt"
                   "alt_kinds"   (as) kind "can" ONLY: the alternative
                                 credential kinds this caller can also consume
                                 for the same operation (today: {"mrz"}).
                                 Sending it makes the dialog offer an in-dialog
                                 switch to that form. Purely additive: a
                                 prompter that does not know the key ignores it
                                 and answers "ok" as before; a caller that does
                                 not send it never gets the switch — and hence
                                 never the "ok_mrz" status below. Unknown
                                 members are ignored, never rendered. On any
                                 other kind the key has no effect.

      Outputs:
        status        "ok" | "ok_mrz" | "cancelled" | "timeout" | "error" |
                      "unauthorized"
                      ("timeout" is answered when, and only when, the window
                      closed itself because "deadline_ms" elapsed with no entry.
                      It is deliberately NOT "cancelled": the holder did not
                      dismiss anything.
                      ("unauthorized" is the fail-closed rejection of a caller
                      whose peer credentials are not the agent — a non-agent
                      process trying to drive the prompter directly; the agent,
                      the only authorized caller, never receives it.
                      "ok_mrz" is returned ONLY to a caller that sent
                      "alt_kinds" containing "mrz" on a "can" request AND whose
                      user switched to the MRZ form before accepting: secret_fd
                      then carries an MRZ payload instead of a CAN. Every other
                      caller — including one that opted in but stayed on the
                      CAN form — keeps the "ok" vocabulary unchanged.)
        secret_fd     sealed memfd containing the secret bytes (size() can be 0
                      on cancellation/error); recipient MUST read, scrub into a
                      Secure::String, and close immediately
        user_message  optional localised explanation (empty on ok)
    -->
    <method name="RequestSecret">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Method.Async" value="server"/>
      <arg name="kind"         type="s"    direction="in"/>
      <arg name="options"      type="a{sv}" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status"       type="s"    direction="out"/>
      <arg name="secret_fd"    type="h"    direction="out"/>
      <arg name="user_message" type="s"    direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      RequestSecrets: ask the user for a related PAIR of secret values in one
      dialog. The only kind so far is the PIN change prompt.

      Inputs:
        kind     "change_pin" — current PIN + new PIN + confirmation. The
                 confirmation is validated inside the prompter and never
                 crosses this interface.
        options  optional metadata; recognises the RequestSecret display keys
                 ("title", "description", "requester", "artifact",
                 "prompt_id", "deadline_ms", "reader_model",
                 "reader_interface", "reader_full") plus:
                   "primary_min_length" (u) bounds of the current-PIN field
                   "primary_max_length" (u)
                   "new_min_length"     (u) bounds of the new AND confirm fields
                   "new_max_length"     (u)
                   "card_label"         (s) human-readable card name
                   "pin_label"          (s) human-readable PIN role name

      Outputs:
        status        same vocabulary as RequestSecret ("ok" | "cancelled" |
                      "timeout" | "error" | "unauthorized")
        primary_fd    sealed memfd with the CURRENT secret; zero-length
                      whenever status is not "ok"
        secondary_fd  sealed memfd with the NEW secret; zero-length whenever
                      status is not "ok"
        user_message  optional localised explanation (empty on ok)

      Both fds follow the RequestSecret rules: always real descriptors,
      sealed before hand-off; the recipient MUST read, scrub into
      Secure::Strings, and close immediately.
    -->
    <method name="RequestSecrets">
      <annotation name="org.freedesktop.DBus.Method.Async" value="server"/>
      <arg name="kind"         type="s"    direction="in"/>
      <arg name="options"      type="a{sv}" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status"       type="s"    direction="out"/>
      <arg name="primary_fd"   type="h"    direction="out"/>
      <arg name="secondary_fd" type="h"    direction="out"/>
      <arg name="user_message" type="s"    direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Dismiss the ONE prompt named by prompt_id. Its in-flight RequestSecret or
      RequestSecrets call returns immediately with status = "cancelled", an
      empty user_message, and — for RequestSecrets — two zero-length sealed fds.
      No other prompt is touched: more than one credential window can stand at
      once, and dismissing "whatever is current" would close another reader's.

      prompt_id is the opaque value the caller sent in the request's options
      dictionary; the prompter never parses it. Idempotent: an id that names no
      live prompt is a silent no-op, so a dismissal racing the user's own is
      harmless.
    -->
    <!--
      The Prompter1 contract this binary implements. The agent reads it ONCE on
      first use and refuses to raise a prompt it could not dismiss, rather than
      leaving a window hanging with nobody able to close it.

      This exists because the prompter is a long-lived user unit and survives an
      agent restart, so a new agent routinely meets an older helper. A helper
      whose version is below what the agent needs is refused loudly, never
      emulated: there is no compatibility shim.
    -->
    <!--
      Close EVERY window this helper is showing, whoever raised it. The agent
      calls it once at startup: a helper outlives an agent restart, and the new
      agent can neither answer nor dismiss windows its predecessor raised, so a
      holder would watch a dead dialog count down for the rest of its entry time.

      Deliberately NOT gated on per-window ownership, unlike Cancel: an orphan's
      owner is a process that no longer exists, so a per-owner sweep would clear
      nothing. The binary-identity gate still applies.

      Each closed window answers its own caller with status = "cancelled".
      Idempotent: resetting an idle helper is a no-op.

      This is a cleanup, not a guarantee. On the one upgrade where orphans are
      most likely the running helper is the OLD one, which understands neither
      this method nor Cancel; there the call simply fails and the version guard
      refuses new prompts. Each window's own entry deadline remains the only
      universal backstop.
    -->
    <method name="Reset"/>

    <property name="ProtocolVersion" type="u" access="read"/>

    <method name="Cancel">
      <arg name="prompt_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
